103	122	\N	2024-12-23 12:36:23.825	87.50	[{"id": 1, "kind": "122", "path": "/mnt/core-data/appfiles/pgpg/uploadedFiles/2\\\\130\\\\SESSION_122_1.pdf", "size": null, "shown": true, "status": null, "sujetPV": null, "dateDepot": null, "dateUpload": "24-02-2025 20:40:13", "pvDocument": null, "responsable": null, "originalName": "Dissertation Anglais.pdf", "dateTelechargement": null}]	DONE	<p>For the past few years, climate change has been one of the consistent topics we hear on the news. Experts have shown that this issue is affecting everyone on this planet and that procedures must be put in place in order to minimize its effects worldwide. Many countries started taking mesures to reduce this phenomenon, some more than others, which led people to question why is the United States should be implementing such changes while many developing nations are disregarding this notice and still contibuting to the problem. Should the developed nations limit their engagement for their industries to be competitive or should growing counties also act towards a greener planet ?</p>\n<p>Most developed countries, with thriving economies and millions of dollars to spare, began treating this matter seriously as they adopted measures to combat climate change, the mass spread of electric vehicules is a most common approach countries used to reduce carbon dyoxide emissions. By lowering taxes on EVs, they encourage consumers to opt for a more environment friendly ways of transportation, moreover some nations offer deals to customers to exchange their gas powered cars for brand new electric ones and invest in compaigns encouraging people to commute together. All of these acts are done to raise awareness of the severity of what this situation can lead to and to lead by example so other nations follow the same steps.</p>\n<p>Growing nations on the other hand are struggling to match these contributions most likely because of the lack of the necessary resources to implement such solutions or because of the fragile economic state they are in where they find themselves prioritizing essential needs of citizens over what can be considered a low priority task on their roadmap. Although this is true for most developing countries, some have started treating this problem, if we take Morroco as an example they built the largest solar farm in Africa taking advantage of their vast desert to produce clean sustainable energy for their country and even their surrounding nations in the future.</p>\n<p>As this is a global matter and is affecting us all, some would say that developed countries should partner with emerging ones in order to help them reach their goals and guide them through this process wether by providing logistics or by offering investements.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, developed countries should treating climate change as a serious threat as they are the most capable of bringing change, moreover other nations should rise to this problem as it is affecting us all and everyone should do their part in preventing its outcome. Even though not everyone will be contributing with the same amount of effort, we should unite to limit climate change outbursts on our planet.</p>	SOUMETTRE	\N	1
